!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math::Real-Numbers
!tags: Math Real-Numbers Bound Extrema

!id: dM9QikMN0C
!front: What does it mean for a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) to have an upper bound, a lower bound, and to be bounded?
!back:
- \( M \in \mathbb{R} \) is an **upper bound** of \( A \) if \( a \le M \) for all \( a \in A \); \( A \) is then **bounded above**.
- \( m \in \mathbb{R} \) is a **lower bound** of \( A \) if \( a \ge m \) for all \( a \in A \); \( A \) is then **bounded below**.
- \( A \) is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below.
- If \( A \) is not bounded, it is **unbounded**.
!extra: ![Anatomy of sup and inf](img/sup_inf_anatomy.svg)
~~~

!id: 0ern8qyuMZ
!front: Give the precise definition of the supremum (least upper bound) of a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
\( s = \sup A \) if:

- \( s \) is an upper bound of \( A \), and
- \( s \le u \) for every upper bound \( u \) of \( A \) (no smaller upper bound exists).
!extra:
Equivalently, \( \sup A \) is the smallest element of the set of upper bounds of \( A \).

![Anatomy of sup and inf](img/sup_inf_anatomy.svg)
~~~

!id: VtVqJTuax4
!front: Give the precise definition of the infimum (greatest lower bound) of a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
\( t = \inf A \) if:

- \( t \) is a lower bound of \( A \), and
- \( t \ge \ell \) for every lower bound \( \ell \) of \( A \) (no larger lower bound exists).
!extra:
Equivalently, \( \inf A \) is the largest element of the set of lower bounds of \( A \).

![Anatomy of sup and inf](img/sup_inf_anatomy.svg)
~~~

!id: yThN4F1NtW
!front: State the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of \( s = \sup A \), equivalent to the definition of least upper bound.
!back:
\( s = \sup A \) if and only if:

- \( s \) is an upper bound of \( A \), and
- for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \), there exists \( x \in A \) such that \( x > s - \varepsilon \).
!extra: The second condition says no upper bound smaller than \( s \) works: points of \( A \) get arbitrarily close to \( s \) from below.
~~~

!id: Z3YjYIB0hd
!front: State the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of \( t = \inf A \), equivalent to the definition of greatest lower bound.
!back:
\( t = \inf A \) if and only if:

- \( t \) is a lower bound of \( A \), and
- for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \), there exists \( x \in A \) such that \( x < t + \varepsilon \).
~~~

!id: UtffdAGybs
!front: Why is the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of \( \sup A \) equivalent to "no upper bound smaller than \( s \) exists"?
!back:
Let:

- \( s \) be an upper bound of \( A \).

To prove: \( s \) is the least upper bound iff for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \) there exists \( x \in A \) with \( x > s - \varepsilon \).

- Fix \( \varepsilon > 0 \) and consider the candidate upper bound \( u = s - \varepsilon < s \).
- "No upper bound smaller than \( s \) exists" means \( u \) fails to be an upper bound of \( A \), i.e. some \( x \in A \) has \( x > u = s - \varepsilon \).
- This holds for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \) exactly when it holds for every candidate \( u < s \), since every \( u < s \) equals \( s - \varepsilon \) for some \( \varepsilon > 0 \).
- So the \( \varepsilon \) condition is just a restatement, ranging over \( \varepsilon > 0 \), of "every real number less than \( s \) fails to be an upper bound."
~~~

!id: Va1Dtp0DfY
!front: Why is the supremum of a set, when it exists, unique?
!back:
Let:

- \( s_1 \) and \( s_2 \) both satisfy the definition of \( \sup A \).

To prove: \( s_1 = s_2 \).

- Since \( s_1 = \sup A \) and \( s_2 \) is an upper bound of \( A \), \( s_1 \le s_2 \).
- Since \( s_2 = \sup A \) and \( s_1 \) is an upper bound of \( A \), \( s_2 \le s_1 \).
- Hence \( s_1 = s_2 \).
!extra: The identical antisymmetry argument, with inequalities reversed, shows \( \inf A \) is unique.
~~~

!id: WXd103Ro6P
!front: Why does \( A \subseteq B \) (both nonempty, \( B \) bounded above) imply \( \sup A \le \sup B \)?
!back: Every upper bound of \( B \) is also an upper bound of \( A \), since \( A \subseteq B \). In particular \( \sup B \) is an upper bound of \( A \), so \( \sup A \), being the *least* upper bound of \( A \), satisfies \( \sup A \le \sup B \).
~~~

!id: pYfrB8LVem
!front: Derive \( \sup(A+B) = \sup A + \sup B \) for nonempty \( A, B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) bounded above, where \( A + B = \{a + b : a \in A, b \in B\} \).
!back:
Let:

- \( s = \sup A \), \( t = \sup B \).

To prove: \( \sup(A+B) = s + t \).

- (\( \le \)) For any \( a \in A, b \in B \), \( a \le s \) and \( b \le t \), so \( a + b \le s + t \); thus \( s+t \) is an upper bound of \( A+B \), giving \( \sup(A+B) \le s+t \).
- (\( \ge \)) Fix \( \varepsilon > 0 \). By the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of sup, there exist \( a \in A \) with \( a > s - \varepsilon/2 \) and \( b \in B \) with \( b > t - \varepsilon/2 \), so \( a+b \in A+B \) satisfies \( a+b > s+t-\varepsilon \).
- Since this holds for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \), no upper bound of \( A+B \) can be smaller than \( s+t \), so \( \sup(A+B) \ge s+t \).
- Combining both inequalities, \( \sup(A+B) = s+t \).
~~~

!id: N4coqnUV2D
!front: How do \( \max A \) and \( \sup A \) relate?
!back:
\( \max A \) exists exactly when \( \sup A \in A \), in which case \( \max A = \sup A \).

\( \sup A \) is the more general notion: it exists whenever \( A \) is nonempty and bounded above, whether or not that bound is attained by an element of \( A \), while \( \max A \) additionally requires the bound to be attained.
~~~

!id: i46iIkjGJK
!front: Does \( \max A \) existing imply that \( \sup A \) exists? If so, how are they related?
!back:
Let:

- \( \max A \) exist, i.e. \( \max A \in A \) and \( \max A \ge a \) for all \( a \in A \).

To prove: \( \sup A \) exists and \( \sup A = \max A \).

- \( \max A \) is itself an upper bound of \( A \), by definition.
- Any upper bound \( u \) of \( A \) satisfies \( u \ge \max A \), since \( \max A \in A \).
- So \( \max A \) is the least upper bound: \( \sup A = \max A \).
!extra: The same argument, with inequalities reversed, shows \( \min A \) existing implies \( \inf A = \min A \). This holds unconditionally, without invoking the completeness axiom — the existence of \( \max A \) is itself the witness that \( \sup A \) exists.
~~~

!id: 280YevvCjE
!front: Does \( \sup A \) existing imply \( \max A \) exists? Construct a counterexample.
!back:
No. Let \( A = \{-\tfrac{1}{n} : n \in \mathbb{N}\} = \{-1, -\tfrac12, -\tfrac13, \dots\} \).

- \( A \) is bounded above by \( 0 \), and \( \sup A = 0 \): by the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization, for any \( \varepsilon > 0 \), some \( n \) has \( -1/n > -\varepsilon \).
- But \( 0 \notin A \), so no element of \( A \) attains the bound: \( \max A \) does not exist.
!extra:
This shows \( \sup \) is strictly weaker than \( \max \): it only requires an upper bound approached arbitrarily closely, not attained. Dually, \( B = \{\tfrac1n : n \in \mathbb{N}\} \) has \( \inf B = 0 \) with no \( \min B \).

![-1/n approaches 0, which is sup A but not in A](img/sup_without_max.svg)
~~~

!id: ll8npMoMHp
!front: Does every nonempty finite subset of \( \mathbb{R} \) have a maximum and minimum?
!back: Yes. Every nonempty finite subset of \( \mathbb{R} \) has both a maximum and a minimum, so \( \sup A = \max A \) and \( \inf A = \min A \) always hold for finite \( A \).
!extra: This contrasts with infinite sets, where attainment can fail even when the set is bounded — e.g. \( \sup(0,1) = 1 \) is never attained, since \( (0,1) \) is infinite.
~~~

!id: Aau27Ct7mq
!front: Find \( \sup(0,1) \) and \( \inf(0,1) \), and state whether each is attained. Contrast with \( [0,1] \).
!back:
\( \sup(0,1) = 1 \) and \( \inf(0,1) = 0 \), neither attained — so \( (0,1) \) has no maximum and no minimum.

For the closed interval, \( \sup[0,1] = \max[0,1] = 1 \) and \( \inf[0,1] = \min[0,1] = 0 \): both bounds are attained since the endpoints belong to the set.
!extra: ![Open interval, not attained, vs. closed interval, attained](img/interval_attainment.svg)
~~~
